<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The station pop-up's banner (#88).

  THE FIRST RESOURCE THIS APP HAS EVER HAD. `android/app/src/main/` held only a
  manifest and a Java tree until now, and `build.rs` has no aapt2 step at all —
  it compiles Java and dexes it, nothing more. So this file exists only in the
  GRADLE build, and `CarnyxAlert` looks it up by NAME at run time rather than
  through `R`: under cargo-apk it is simply absent, `getIdentifier` answers 0,
  and the notification falls back to the platform's own template. That is the
  same shape as `CarnyxService`, which is also Gradle-only, and it is why no
  build breaks for want of a layout.

  WHAT IT REPLACES, AND WHY. The standard template puts a large icon in a small
  square at the banner's right edge. Station logos are landscape wordmarks — the
  three in the handoff are 408x296, 545x200 and 255x144 — so one fitted to that
  slot leaves the station's name a few pixels tall. Given the card's own width
  the same mark is legible, which is the whole reason for a custom view.

  ── THE VIEWS ARE NOT A FREE CHOICE ──
  Only classes marked `@RemoteView` inflate in the shade's process: the four
  plain layouts, TextView, ImageView, Button, ImageButton, ProgressBar,
  Chronometer, ViewFlipper and the list types. No ConstraintLayout, no custom
  views, and only the fixed set of `setX` operations `RemoteViews` exposes.

  ── COLOURS COME FROM ATTRIBUTES, NOT FROM HEX ──
  This is inflated by SystemUI against the NOTIFICATION theme, which is light or
  dark depending on the shade — and a hard-coded `#1C2028` is invisible on half
  of them. `?android:attr/textColorPrimary` and `textColorSecondary` are public
  platform attributes and resolve to whatever that theme says, which is the only
  way a custom notification layout survives both.
-->
<LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:orientation="horizontal"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
    android:paddingStart="16dp"
    android:paddingEnd="16dp"
    android:paddingTop="8dp"
    android:paddingBottom="12dp"
    android:baselineAligned="false"
    android:gravity="center">

    <!--
      GONE RATHER THAN INVISIBLE when the station has no saved logo. `gone` takes
      the view out of the measure entirely, so the two lines centre in the card
      on their own; `invisible` would keep its width and leave them shoved off
      centre by an empty slot.

      `adjustViewBounds` with a fixed HEIGHT and `wrap_content` width is what
      preserves a landscape mark's aspect: the height is the constraint and the
      width follows from it, up to `maxWidth` for a mark far wider than it is
      tall.
    -->
    <ImageView
        android:id="@+id/popup_logo"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="56dp"
        android:maxWidth="180dp"
        android:adjustViewBounds="true"
        android:scaleType="fitCenter"
        android:layout_marginEnd="18dp"
        android:contentDescription="@null"
        android:visibility="gone" />

    <!--
      `wrap_content` AND NO WEIGHT, which is the whole of the centring. A column
      at `0dp` with `layout_weight="1"` swallows every remaining pixel, so the
      group always spans the card and `gravity` has nothing left to centre. Sized
      to its contents, the group is as wide as the logo and the words together
      and the root's `center` puts it in the middle.

      The cost is that a string wider than the card CLIPS rather than elides.
      Nothing sent here can be: a call sign is four characters and a dial is six.
      A second line carrying a station's long name would need `ellipsize` and a
      `maxWidth` back.

      For the two lines centred on each other rather than ranged left, add
      `android:gravity="center_horizontal"` here.
    -->
    <LinearLayout
        android:orientation="vertical"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content">

        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/popup_call"
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:singleLine="true"
            android:textSize="17sp"
            android:textStyle="bold"
            android:textColor="?android:attr/textColorPrimary" />

        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/popup_dial"
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:singleLine="true"
            android:textSize="15sp"
            android:textColor="?android:attr/textColorSecondary" />
    </LinearLayout>
</LinearLayout>
